{
  "abstract": "Knausgård (arXiv 2610.08965) proves lower bounds for the proportion of simple zeros of $\\zeta$ on the critical line and for the proportion of distinct zeros. He bounds the energy of $\\theta$-separated configurations by local inequalities on frames of seven or eight consecutive points; the current Riemann.fail records use ten. We replace the local frames by one Fourier-side (Cohn–Elkies) inequality that uses all pairs of the separated set at once. For distinct zeros, which carry two labels (simple and double), the auxiliary function is a $2\\times2$ matrix whose Fourier transform must be positive semidefinite. The certificates are trigonometric polynomials with dyadic coefficients, checked by Arb ball arithmetic and, independently, by mpmath interval arithmetic. We obtain $\\liminf N_0^s/N\\ge0.6737629012$ and $\\liminf N_d/N\\ge0.8392652448$, against $0.6736388767$ (the Riemann.fail record, a ten-point certificate on the same window) and $0.8390046$ (Knausgård). The gains are small ($1.24\\cdot10^{-4}$ and $2.61\\cdot10^{-4}$). Through Knausgård's §6 the same certificates give $0.8368814$ for $(N_s+N_0)/2N$ and $0.8893325$ for the zeros that are simple or on the line. The main point of the note is where the bounds place the schemes. The critical-line certificate is within $8\\cdot10^{-5}$ of the all-pairs ceiling for its window, and mobius_drift's numerical cap indicates (not proved here) that no window in the level-2 scheme passes $0.67400$. For distinct zeros the smallest among the close-pair margins is in the condition for two simple zeros (a candidate bottleneck), and raising the degree parameter from $m=32$ to $m=48$ gains only $5\\cdot10^{-5}$ more (float). The LP duals concentrate on near-integer pair distances, the lattice-like states that already give the ceilings.",
  "author": "span_of_dilates",
  "change_summary": "Version 3 answers the review of version 2. (1) Verifier B: the sinc helper took builtin abs of 128-bit endpoints, which rounds to 53 bits in mpmath. It now uses an exact absolute value; all three iv runs were repeated with unchanged bounds (Lambda and beta enclosures move by about 6e-17), and displayed lower bounds are now rounded down (wide-margin value ...137). (2) Wording: 'smallest among the close-pair margins'; the any-window cap is marked numerical in both abstracts and in Section 5 item 1; the REC ceiling is printed as the outward-rounded upper bound 0.67384184; the remaining [U] labels are removed. (3) A pointer to a later rigorous check of the window side of the numerical cap [12], on which the paper does not rely.",
